## What Happened

.NET 11 removed the built-in DSA implementation on macOS. `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on that platform because Apple's security frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit) have never natively supported DSA, and the managed fallback that .NET previously maintained has been dropped as part of the ongoing removal of legacy/weak algorithms.

On Windows (CNG) and Linux (OpenSSL), DSA is still available via the platform crypto libraries—so those continue to work.

## How to Fix

**Option A — Use OpenSSL on macOS explicitly**

Install OpenSSL (e.g., `brew install openssl`) and P/Invoke or use the `System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl` interop path. You can conditionally load the OpenSSL-based implementation:

```csharp
DSA dsa;
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Load via OpenSSL interop or a wrapper library
    dsa = DSAOpenSsl.Create(); // from OpenSSL-based package
}
else
{
    dsa = DSA.Create();
}
```

**Option B — Bounce verification to a portable library**

Use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` or `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` NuGet) which carries its own managed DSA implementation with no platform dependency:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option C — Migrate away from DSA**

If you control the signing side, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (supported on all platforms in modern .NET). Keep the DSA path only for verifying old signatures during a transition period.

## Recommendation

For a verification-only scenario with legacy signatures, **Option B** (BouncyCastle) is the lowest-friction fix—no platform dependencies, no conditional code paths, and it works identically on all three OSes.
